  casestudy:{title:"A law as a historical source",text:"A classroom example describes an ancient law prescribing different penalties according to social status. A learner concludes that because the rules were written down, everyone enjoyed equal rights.",questions:[{q:"What does a written law collection show?",a:"How an authority represented justice, obligations and social distinctions."},{q:"Why is the learner's conclusion unsupported?",a:"Written laws can preserve inequality, and the described penalties explicitly distinguish status."},{q:"What further question should a historian ask?",a:"How laws were enforced in practice and whose experiences are missing from the surviving text."}]},
